WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:02.959
So I just want to clarify
for everyone watching us

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.708
that as a child
you were diagnosed

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:07.667
with an auditory
processing disorder

00:00:07.708 --> 00:00:08.959
and a speech impediment.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.166
Can you explain what that is

00:00:11.208 --> 00:00:13.166
and how it shaped your life?

00:00:13.208 --> 00:00:16.166
Because now
you speak perfectly,

00:00:16.208 --> 00:00:21.375
and it's hard to imagine
what you were before,

00:00:21.417 --> 00:00:23.166
what your speech
was like before.

00:00:23.208 --> 00:00:25.792
Well, I was born early,
along with my twin,

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:28.375
and a lot of times for infants

00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:30.375
that can lead
to learning delays.

00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:32.959
And so one of my delays
was in speech

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.583
and speech pronunciation,

00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:37.166
and also just
the auditory processing issue

00:00:37.208 --> 00:00:39.166
just means
that I really struggle

00:00:39.208 --> 00:00:40.792
as an auditory learner.

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:44.792
But what that also meant
is the learning process

00:00:44.834 --> 00:00:48.583
by which at a certain age
kids are saying "R"

00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:51.375
and "shh" and "chh,"
those were a lot of the sounds

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:52.959
that I would still
struggle with--

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.959
specifically "R" because
it is one of the most complex

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.166
letters in
the English alphabet.

00:00:58.208 --> 00:01:00.583
That was something
that I would struggle with

00:01:00.625 --> 00:01:03.333
until probably
20 years of age.

00:01:03.375 --> 00:01:06.583
And when you have
a last name like Gorman,

00:01:06.625 --> 00:01:09.583
when you are writing poetry,

00:01:09.625 --> 00:01:11.583
all the things
that constitute my identity,

00:01:11.625 --> 00:01:13.583
when you go
to a school like Harvard,

00:01:13.625 --> 00:01:15.166
which has two Rs in it,

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:18.166
it leads to all of these
kind of road blocks.

00:01:18.208 --> 00:01:20.959
But I'm really grateful,
actually, for that experience,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.792
because it informs my poetry
and I think it made me

00:01:23.834 --> 00:01:25.959
all that much stronger
of a writer.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.583
When you have to teach yourself
how to say words from scratch,

00:01:29.625 --> 00:01:33.583
when you are learning through
poetry how to speak English,

00:01:33.625 --> 00:01:36.583
it lent to a great
understanding, I think,

00:01:36.625 --> 00:01:39.792
of sound, of pitch,
of pronunciation.

00:01:39.834 --> 00:01:42.166
So, I think of
my speech impediment

00:01:42.208 --> 00:01:45.166
not as a weakness
or a disability,

00:01:45.208 --> 00:01:46.959
but as one of
my greatest strengths.

